<Layout title="Home">

  <section class="intro">
    <div>
      <h1>Abhishek Karna</h1>
      <p class="role">PhD student in physics, Princeton University</p>

      <!-- Introduction: replace with your own text. -->
      <p>
        I work on theoretical quantum condensed matter physics and machine learning for quantum materials with
        <a href="https://bernevig-group.github.io" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">B. Andrei Bernevig</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Before Princeton I earned a BS in
        <a href="https://physics.duke.edu" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Physics</a> and a BS in
        <a href="https://math.duke.edu" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Mathematics</a> at Duke University,
        with a certificate in
        <a href="https://dukeppe.trinity.duke.edu/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Philosophy, Politics and Economics</a>.
        I graduated with
        <a href="https://physics.duke.edu/physics-graduation-2026" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">High Distinction in Physics</a>,
        the department's highest honor. At Duke I developed a
        <a href="https://journals.aps.org/prd/abstract/10.1103/gtbl-vsdt" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">quantum Monte Carlo method for lattice Hamiltonians</a>
        with <a href="https://scholars.duke.edu/person/sch" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Shailesh Chandrasekharan</a>,
        and synthesized and grew crystals of quantum magnets in
        <a href="https://scholars.duke.edu/person/Sara.Haravifard" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sara Haravifard</a>'s lab.
        I also worked on <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.apr.2026.102920" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">air pollution in Nepal</a>
        with <a href="https://scholars.duke.edu/person/brian.mcadoo" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Brian McAdoo</a>
        at <a href="https://planetlab.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PlanetLab</a>.
      </p>

